THOUGHT: `applications` is an `AtomicReference<Applications>` initialized with `new Applications()`, but later code (e.g., `fetchRemoteRegistry`) can set it to `null`, so NullAway correctly flags `applications.get()` as nullable. In `getApplication`, the safest fix is to avoid dereferencing a potentially null `Applications` and instead delegate to `getApplications()`, which already encapsulates the current value. I’ll update `getApplication` to call `getApplications()` and handle a possible null by returning null (matching other nullable-returning patterns in the class), then run the NullAway build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/eureka && sed -i '526,529s/@Override
  public Application getApplication(String appName) {
    return this.applications.get().getRegisteredApplications(appName);
  }/@Override
  @Nullable
  public Application getApplication(String appName) {
    Applications apps = getApplications();
    return (apps == null) ? null : apps.getRegisteredApplications(appName);
  }/' eureka-core/src/main/java/com/netflix/eureka/registry/RemoteRegionRegistry.java && ./gradlew eureka-core:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -n "RemoteRegionRegistry.java" new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log
```

